---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const shakeCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'n', label: 'people in the room n', min: 2, max: 20, step: 1, value: 10 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'handshakes (every pair once)', expr: 'n*(n-1)/2' },
    { label: 'ordered passings (n×(n−1))', expr: 'n*(n-1)' },
    { label: 'if one leader shakes everyone', expr: 'n-1' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/intermediate/counting-and-combinatorics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Combinatorics is counting without counting — replacing exhausting lists with structural
    arguments. Two principles do most of the work: the <strong>multiplication rule</strong>
    (independent choices multiply: 3 shirts × 4 trousers = 12 outfits) and the
    <strong>subtraction principle</strong> (count the complement when it's easier — the
    probability lesson uses this constantly).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the handshake problem — order doesn't matter, so divide the ordered count by 2" widget="sliderLab" config={shakeCfg} />

  <p>
    Every pair shakes hands once, but n×(n−1) counts each pair twice (AB and BA). Divide by 2:
    n(n−1)/2. That divide-by-ordering move is the whole difference between
    <strong>permutations</strong> (order matters) and <strong>combinations</strong> (it doesn't):
    C(n,r) = P(n,r)/r!.
  </p>

  <h2>The toolkit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Permutations:</strong> n!/(n−r)! — arrangements where order matters (podiums, passwords, batting orders).</li>
    <li><strong>Combinations:</strong> n!/(r!(n−r)!) — selections where it doesn't (committees, poker hands, lottery tickets).</li>
    <li><strong>Pascal's triangle</strong> encodes all of them, and its rows sum to powers of 2 — the bridge to the binomial theorem.</li>
    <li><strong>Pigeonhole principle:</strong> n+1 pigeons in n holes forces a collision. It proves non-obvious things — like two people in London have exactly the same number of hairs on their head.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Combinatorics feeds directly into probability (counting favourable cases), computer science
    (why some problems explode combinatorially), and the statistics subject's sampling theory.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Contemporary Mathematics (Combinatorics); standard discrete mathematics texts (Rosen).</p>
</TopicPage>
